WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_adp_111'), 'Before: GET /courses/crs_42 returned
{"id":"crs_42","title":"HTTP Basics","level":"beginner","price":149000,"prerequisites":["crs_10"],"certificate":true}

Admin app 2.3 sent:
PUT /courses/crs_42
Content-Type: application/json

{"title":"HTTP Fundamentals","description":"...","level":"beginner","price":149000}

Response: 200 OK
{"id":"crs_42","title":"HTTP Fundamentals","level":"beginner","price":149000,"prerequisites":[],"certificate":false}

Statement: "The server treated this PUT the way HTTP defines it, and the same edit sent as PATCH with Content-Type application/merge-patch+json and the body {\"title\":\"HTTP Fundamentals\"} would have kept the prerequisites and the certificate setting."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. RFC 9110 defines PUT as replacing the state of the target resource with the state in the request body, so a member the body does not contain is not kept: the server reset prerequisites and certificate to their defaults because admin app 2.3 has never heard of them. A JSON merge patch (RFC 7396) works the other way round: members absent from the patch are left unchanged, so a patch with only the new title changes only the title. The misconception is that PUT means "update whatever I send"; that is what PATCH with a merge patch means, and it is why edits from forms that an older client builds belong in PATCH.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_adp_111') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
