{"id":"crs_42","title":"HTTP Fundamentals","tags":["http","api"],"discountPrice":99000}

The new course editor sent:
PATCH /courses/crs_42
Content-Type: application/merge-patch+json

{"tags":["http"],"discountPrice":null}

The server applies the patch with the RFC 7396 algorithm and answers 200 OK.

Statement: "After this request the course still has the tag \"api\", because a merge patch only adds or replaces the array elements it lists, and discountPrice is still 99000, because null members in a merge patch are skipped."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False, on both counts. RFC 7396 merges objects member by member, but any value that is not an object, arrays included, simply replaces the current value: tags becomes ["http"] and the tag "api" is gone. A member set to null in a merge patch removes that member, so discountPrice is removed (or reset to its default if the schema requires it). The misconception is to read a merge patch as "merge everything": it merges objects only, and null is an instruction, not a placeholder. To add one tag or to set a value to null you need JSON Patch (RFC 6902) or a dedicated endpoint.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_adp_111') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
