WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_adp_121'), 'A learner''s token (not an instructor) sends three requests:

GET /courses/crs_draft_77  -> 403 Forbidden  {"code":"FORBIDDEN"}
GET /courses/crs_draft_78  -> 404 Not Found  {"code":"COURSE_NOT_FOUND"}
GET /courses/crs_nope      -> 404 Not Found  {"code":"COURSE_NOT_FOUND"}

crs_draft_77 is an unpublished draft; crs_draft_78 and crs_nope do not exist.

Statement: "Without seeing any course content, this learner can tell from the status codes alone that a course with the id crs_draft_77 exists."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. 403 means "I know who you are and you may not access this resource", which confirms the resource exists; 404 means there is nothing there for this caller. Trying ids and keeping the 403s lists every draft, which is how the forum thread found upcoming courses. RFC 9110 lets a server that wants to hide a forbidden resource answer 404 instead, and the hidden and the missing answer must then be identical, body and headers included. The misconception is that 403 is always the "more correct" answer for a resource the caller may not see: it is correct only when the caller is allowed to know the resource exists.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_adp_121') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
