Statement: "A contract test that checks real API responses against two schemas - what Android 3.8 decodes (rating key present, instructor an object, price an integer) and what the web app decodes - would have failed on release 2026.10.22-2 and passes after the expand fix."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. On 2026.10.22-2 the old-client schema fails on every course (rating and instructor missing, price a string), so the test fails before the deploy. After the expand fix both schemas pass, because each response carries the old and the new fields. Validating real responses also catches what the OpenAPI diff in E cannot: a mapper that disagrees with the document. The test has to stay until the contract step, so nobody removes rating early. The misconception is to verify a compatibility fix by opening the app once on a test phone: one device checks one version, the schemas check every response.', 6, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_adp_411') AND order_index = 6)
    RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO challenge.question_options (question_id, option_text, is_correct)
SELECT q.id, o.option_text, o.is_correct
FROM q, (VALUES
        ('True', true),
        ('False', false)
    ) AS o(option_text, is_correct);

-- challenge chal_coding_adp_412 (fix_bug)
INSERT INTO challenge.challenges (public_id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, name, title, description, status, difficulty, type, points, is_published, is_deleted)
VALUES ('chal_coding_adp_412', (SELECT id FROM catalog.course WHERE public_id = 'api-design-production'), (SELECT id FROM course.module WHERE course_id = (SELECT id FROM catalog.course WHERE public_id = 'api-design-production') AND order_index = 4 AND is_deleted = false LIMIT 1), (SELECT id FROM course.lesson WHERE content_source = 'lesson-adp-4-1' AND is_deleted = false LIMIT 1), 'ship-a-breaking-change-safely', 'Ship a Breaking Change Safely', 'Since CodeDev API release 2026.10.22-2 the Android app 3.8 (40% of Android users, cannot be force-updated for two weeks) crashes on every catalog screen with "Fields [rating, instructor] are required", while the web app, deployed together with the API, works. The release notes say "renamed rating to averageRating, cleaned up the course DTO". Fix the course mapper and add the deprecation headers so that both clients work during the migration.

Every course response must carry: the old, deprecated fields with their old types - `rating` (number or null, the key always present), `instructor` ({ id, name }) and `price` (integer, in dong); the new fields - `averageRating` (number or null, the key always present), `teacher` ({ id, name }) and `priceAmount` (the price in dong as a decimal string without separators, e.g. "149000"); and the unchanged `id`, `slug`, `title` and `ratingCount`.

`deprecationHeaders(policy)` returns the headers sent with every course response: `Deprecation` = "@" followed by `policy.deprecatedAt` in whole Unix seconds, rounded down (the RFC 9745 form, e.g. "@1792746000"); `Sunset` = `policy.sunsetAt` as an HTTP-date such as "Mon, 30 Nov 2026 00:00:00 GMT" (RFC 8594), and no `Sunset` key at all when `policy.sunsetAt` is null. It throws an Error when a date cannot be parsed, or when `sunsetAt` is earlier than 14 days after `deprecatedAt` (exactly 14 days is allowed). Dates are ISO 8601 strings with an offset.

Input (stdin, JSON): { "policy": { "deprecatedAt", "sunsetAt" }, "rows": CourseRow[] }. The test runner (do not change) checks every response against the old-client and the new-client schema and prints { responses, headers, oldClient: { failed, firstErrors }, newClient: { failed, firstErrors } }, or { "error": "invalid deprecation policy" } when deprecationHeaders throws.', 'open', 'medium'::difficulty_question, 'fix_bug', 30, true, false)
ON CONFLICT (public_id) DO UPDATE SET
    course_id = EXCLUDED.course_id, module_id = EXCLUDED.module_id, lesson_id = EXCLUDED.lesson_id, name = EXCLUDED.name,
    title = EXCLUDED.title, description = EXCLUDED.description, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty, type = EXCLUDED.type,
    points = EXCLUDED.points, is_published = true, is_deleted = false, updated_at = now();
DELETE FROM challenge.challenge_hints WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_coding_adp_412');
INSERT INTO challenge.challenge_hints (challenge_id, level, body)
VALUES ((SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_coding_adp_412'), 1, 'Which fields does app 3.8 require, and with which types? A field can only have one type in a response: where can the new string price go so that the old integer price stays?'),
       ((SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_coding_adp_412'), 2, 'Compare toCourseDto and the CourseDto type it returns with the old-client list in the description, field by field. Then look at what deprecationHeaders returns, and at the unit Date.parse returns.'),
       ((SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_coding_adp_412'), 3, 'A similar case: renaming `email` to `contactEmail` on a user response during a migration means returning both `email` and `contactEmail` with the same value. A deprecation date of 2026-01-15T00:00:00Z is 1768435200000 ms after the epoch, so its header value is "@1768435200"; a sunset of 2026-02-01T12:00:00Z is written "Sun, 01 Feb 2026 12:00:00 GMT".');
INSERT INTO challenge.coding_configs (challenge_id, execution_mode, time_limit_ms, memory_limit_mb, max_attempts, scoring_mode)
VALUES ((SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_coding_adp_412'), 'stdio', 3000, 128, NULL, 'all_or_nothing')
ON CONFLICT (challenge_id) DO UPDATE SET
