WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_adp_421'), 'PR #517 "courses: expert level, ratings can be empty" - diff of the generated openapi.json, response of GET /v1/courses/{slug}:
   "level": {
     "type": "string",
-    "enum": ["beginner", "intermediate", "advanced"]
+    "enum": ["beginner", "intermediate", "advanced", "expert"]
   },
   "averageRating": {
-    "type": "number"
+    "type": ["number", "null"]
   },
+  "badges": { "type": "array", "items": { "type": "string" } },

Android 3.9 model (Kotlin, kotlinx.serialization):
enum class Level { @SerialName("beginner") BEGINNER, @SerialName("intermediate") INTERMEDIATE, @SerialName("advanced") ADVANCED }
data class CourseDto(..., val level: Level, val averageRating: Double, ...), decoded with Json { ignoreUnknownKeys = true }

Reviewer''s comment: "Only additions, nothing removed - safe to merge."

Statement: "Adding \"expert\" to the level enum is safe for the Android app, because a value was added and none was removed."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. For a client that reads the response, a new enum value is a value it has never seen. Android 3.9 decodes level into a closed Kotlin enum with three entries, so the first course with level "expert" fails to decode, just like a missing field. Adding an enum value is safe in a request (the server learns to accept more) and breaking in a response read by exhaustive clients, unless every client was built with an unknown-value fallback. The misconception is to judge a change by "added or removed" instead of by who reads it and what they can handle.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_adp_421') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
