    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_adp_421'), 'PR #517 "courses: expert level, ratings can be empty" - diff of the generated openapi.json, response of GET /v1/courses/{slug}:
   "level": {
     "type": "string",
-    "enum": ["beginner", "intermediate", "advanced"]
+    "enum": ["beginner", "intermediate", "advanced", "expert"]
   },
   "averageRating": {
-    "type": "number"
+    "type": ["number", "null"]
   },
+  "badges": { "type": "array", "items": { "type": "string" } },

Android 3.9 model (Kotlin, kotlinx.serialization):
enum class Level { @SerialName("beginner") BEGINNER, @SerialName("intermediate") INTERMEDIATE, @SerialName("advanced") ADVANCED }
data class CourseDto(..., val level: Level, val averageRating: Double, ...), decoded with Json { ignoreUnknownKeys = true }

Reviewer''s comment: "Only additions, nothing removed - safe to merge."

Statement: "Changing averageRating from \"number\" to [\"number\", \"null\"] is a breaking change for Android 3.9, even though the diff only adds a type to the list."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The old schema promised a number in every response, and 3.9 declares averageRating as a non-nullable Double. The first course without reviews sends null and decoding fails. For response readers, a schema that accepts more values is a promise of fewer guarantees: became-nullable is breaking, while the opposite (removing null) is safe. In OpenAPI 3.1 nullability is part of the type list, so a diff that looks like a pure addition can be the breaking change. The new optional badges property, on the other hand, is safe for 3.9, which ignores unknown keys.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_adp_421') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
